{"title":"Discrete Math Foundations","summary":"Discrete Math Foundations covers relations, functions, and order structures — the formal vocabulary used to describe how elements of a set connect to one another. A relation on a set A is any subset of A×A; when a relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation, and it partitions A into disjoint equivalence classes, which is exactly the idea behind grouping elements by a shared property, such as connected components in a graph. A relation that is instead reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive is a partial order, drawn as a Hasse diagram; a poset where every pair of elements has a unique least upper bound (join) and greatest lower bound (meet) is called a lattice. Functions are a restricted kind of relation where every input maps to exactly one output, classified as injective (no two inputs share an output), surjective (every output is hit), or bijective (both — which is what makes a function invertible). These ideas matter beyond pure math: a relation is the mathematical model behind a database table or a graph's edge set, and mathematical induction, the chapter's core proof technique, is the same reasoning used later to prove loop invariants and the correctness of recursive algorithms.","learning_outcomes":["Given a relation on a finite set, determine whether it is reflexive, symmetric, antisymmetric, and/or transitive, and classify it as an equivalence relation, partial order, both, or neither.","Given a finite equivalence relation, construct its equivalence classes and verify they partition the set.","Given a function's mapping table or formula, determine whether it is injective, surjective, and/or bijective, and construct its inverse if one exists."],"common_mistakes":["Treating 'symmetric' and 'antisymmetric' as opposites — they are not mutually exclusive; equality is both, and many relations are neither, so each property must be checked independently against its formal definition.","Assuming a partial order must be a total order — a poset only requires transitivity among comparable pairs, not that every pair be comparable; divisibility on the positive integers is a valid partial order even though 4 and 6 are incomparable.","Declaring a function bijective after checking only a handful of sample inputs — surjectivity is a claim about the entire codomain, and a function that looks one-to-one on a few points can still miss a codomain element or collide elsewhere in the domain.","Forgetting that a property like reflexivity must hold for every element of the set — one missing pair (a,a) invalidates reflexivity for the whole relation, not just for that element."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Let A = {1,2,3,4,5,6} and define relation R by a R b iff a mod 3 = b mod 3. Verify R is an equivalence relation and find its equivalence classes.","steps":["Compute a mod 3 for each element: 1→1, 2→2, 3→0, 4→1, 5→2, 6→0.","Check reflexivity: for any a, a mod 3 = a mod 3, so (a,a) ∈ R for all a ∈ A.","Check symmetry: if a mod 3 = b mod 3 then b mod 3 = a mod 3, so R is symmetric.","Check transitivity: if a mod 3 = b mod 3 and b mod 3 = c mod 3, then a mod 3 = c mod 3, so R is transitive — R is confirmed an equivalence relation.","Group elements by shared remainder: remainder 0 → {3,6}; remainder 1 → {1,4}; remainder 2 → {2,5}."],"result":"R partitions A into exactly 3 equivalence classes — {1,4}, {2,5}, {3,6} — matching the 3 possible remainders mod 3, and their union recovers all of A with no overlap."}}
{"title":"Set Theory & Mathematical Logic","summary":"Set theory and mathematical logic supply the two most basic tools for making precise, checkable statements: sets for describing collections of objects, and propositional/predicate logic for describing and combining truth claims about them. Propositional logic works with statements that are true or false and combines them using AND (∧), OR (∨), NOT (¬), implication (→), and biconditional (↔); every compound statement's truth value can be checked exhaustively with a truth table, and De Morgan's laws — ¬(P∧Q) ≡ ¬P∨¬Q, and ¬(P∨Q) ≡ ¬P∧¬Q — let negation be pushed through a formula, exactly what happens when negating a loop condition or a query predicate in code. Predicate logic extends this with quantifiers, ∀ ('for all') and ∃ ('there exists'), so statements can range over a domain, and negating a quantified statement flips the quantifier and negates the predicate: ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x). Sets are built from these same primitives — union, intersection, complement, and difference mirror OR, AND, NOT, and set difference respectively, and set-builder notation is just predicate logic used to describe membership. This machinery is the backbone of formal specification and database query semantics (SQL's WHERE clause is predicate logic evaluated row by row), and it underlies correctness proofs throughout the rest of the curriculum.","learning_outcomes":["Given a compound propositional formula, construct its full truth table and determine whether it is a tautology, contradiction, or contingency.","Apply De Morgan's laws and other standard logical equivalences to convert a propositional or predicate formula into an equivalent form, including correctly negating a quantified statement.","Translate a natural-language statement involving 'all', 'some', or 'none' into correct predicate logic notation using ∀ and ∃, and identify its correct negation."],"common_mistakes":["Negating '∀x P(x)' as '∀x ¬P(x)' instead of '∃x ¬P(x)' — negation flips the quantifier as well as the predicate, and doing only one half produces a logically different statement.","Treating implication P→Q as equivalent to its converse Q→P — the two are logically different (P→Q is false only when P is true and Q is false), and conflating them is one of the most common errors in argument validity questions.","Misapplying De Morgan's law by distributing negation without flipping the connective — ¬(P∧Q) becomes ¬P∨¬Q, not ¬P∧¬Q; keeping the same connective produces a non-equivalent formula.","Assuming a vacuously true implication is a meaningful claim — an implication with a false antecedent is true by definition regardless of the consequent, which trips up truth-table construction and proofs by contradiction."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Prove ¬(P → Q) ≡ P ∧ ¬Q using a truth table, then use the equivalence to correctly negate the statement 'If n is prime, then n is odd.'","steps":["Build the truth table for P→Q across all 4 combinations of P,Q: (T,T)→T, (T,F)→F, (F,T)→T, (F,F)→T.","Negate each row to get ¬(P→Q): (T,T)→F, (T,F)→T, (F,T)→F, (F,F)→F.","Build the truth table for P∧¬Q, true only when P=T and Q=F: (T,T)→F, (T,F)→T, (F,T)→F, (F,F)→F.","Compare the two truth tables row by row — they match on all four rows, confirming ¬(P→Q) ≡ P∧¬Q.","Apply the equivalence with P='n is prime', Q='n is odd': the correct negation is 'n is prime AND n is not odd', i.e. 'n is prime and n is even' — not the naive flip 'if n is prime then n is even'.","Test n=2: it is prime and even, satisfying the negation and confirming the original implication is false in general, even though it holds for every odd prime."],"result":"¬(P→Q) ≡ P∧¬Q is confirmed by matching truth tables, and applying it correctly identifies n=2 as the counterexample to 'if n is prime, then n is odd' — a case naive negation would miss entirely."}}
{"title":"Combinatorics & Probability","summary":"Combinatorics and probability answer two closely related questions: how many ways can something happen, and how likely is it to happen. Counting rests on two basic rules — the sum rule (if a choice can be made in m ways OR n ways, with no overlap, there are m+n total options) and the product rule (if one choice can be made in m ways and, independently, a second in n ways, there are m×n combined outcomes) — from which permutations (nPr = n!/(n−r)!, order matters) and combinations (nCr = n!/(r!(n−r)!), order doesn't matter) are derived. The pigeonhole principle — if n items are placed into m containers and n>m, at least one container holds more than one item — looks simple but proves surprisingly strong results, such as guaranteeing two people in any group of 13 share a birth month. Probability builds on counting: for equally likely outcomes, P(event) = favorable outcomes / total outcomes, and conditional probability P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B) captures how new information changes the odds of an event, formalized further by Bayes' theorem, P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B), used to update a hypothesis's probability given observed evidence. These tools directly underpin algorithm analysis (expected running time of randomized algorithms like randomized quicksort), hashing (analyzing collision probability), and machine learning (Bayesian classifiers), making this one of the most practically-referenced math topics in the rest of the curriculum.","learning_outcomes":["Given a counting problem, correctly decide whether to apply the sum rule, product rule, permutations, or combinations, and compute the resulting count.","Apply the pigeonhole principle to prove a guaranteed-repetition claim about a finite set of items distributed into a finite set of categories.","Given a conditional probability or Bayes' theorem problem with stated priors and likelihoods, compute the correct posterior probability."],"common_mistakes":["Using a combination formula (nCr) when order actually matters, such as assigning distinct prizes to winners — this undercounts by a factor of r! since combinations discard ordering information the problem requires.","Applying the sum rule to sets that actually overlap — the sum rule only gives m+n directly when the two cases share no outcomes; overlapping cases require inclusion-exclusion, |A∪B| = |A|+|B|−|A∩B|, instead.","Confusing P(A|B) with P(B|A) — these are generally not equal, and swapping them without applying Bayes' theorem produces a wrong posterior probability (the classic base-rate fallacy).","Treating dependent events as independent when computing a joint probability, multiplying P(A)×P(B) instead of P(A)×P(B|A) — this is only valid when A and B are actually independent, and assuming so silently is a common source of wrong answers."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A disease affects 1% of a population (P(D)=0.01). A test has a 95% true positive rate (P(+|D)=0.95) and a 10% false positive rate (P(+|¬D)=0.10). A random person tests positive — find P(D|+).","steps":["Write Bayes' theorem: P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+).","Note P(D) = 0.01, so P(¬D) = 0.99.","Compute the numerator: P(+|D)·P(D) = 0.95 × 0.01 = 0.0095.","Compute P(+) via the law of total probability: P(+) = (0.95×0.01) + (0.10×0.99) = 0.0095 + 0.099 = 0.1085.","Divide: P(D|+) = 0.0095 / 0.1085 ≈ 0.0876."],"result":"P(D|+) ≈ 8.76% — even after a positive test with 95% sensitivity, the person is still more likely not to have the disease, because the disease is rare and the 10% false-positive rate generates far more false alarms than true positives. This is the standard illustration of why P(A|B) ≠ P(B|A)."}}
{"title":"Calculus Basics","summary":"Calculus Basics covers limits, continuity, and differentiation — the formal tools for describing how a function behaves as its input changes, including at points it isn't directly evaluated at. A limit, written lim(x→a) f(x) = L, formalizes the idea that f(x) gets arbitrarily close to L as x approaches a, without necessarily requiring f to be defined at a itself; a function is continuous at a point if the limit exists there, f is defined there, and the two values agree. Differentiability is a stronger condition: f is differentiable at a if its derivative f'(a) = lim(h→0) [f(a+h)−f(a)]/h exists, and differentiability at a point implies continuity there, though the converse is false — |x| is continuous but not differentiable at x=0. Standard derivative rules — power rule, product rule, quotient rule, chain rule — let derivatives of complex expressions be computed mechanically from these primitives, and two central theorems tie the local behavior of a derivative to the function's global behavior: Rolle's theorem (if f is continuous on [a,b], differentiable on (a,b), and f(a)=f(b), then some c in (a,b) has f'(c)=0) and its generalization, the Mean Value Theorem (some c has f'(c) equal to the function's average rate of change over [a,b]). These ideas justify why derivative-based reasoning works at all — for example, that a zero derivative everywhere implies a constant function — which underlies later work in optimization and numerical methods.","learning_outcomes":["Evaluate limits of algebraic, rational, and piecewise functions, including indeterminate forms requiring algebraic simplification or L'Hôpital's rule.","Determine whether a given function is continuous and/or differentiable at a specified point, and justify the answer using the formal definitions.","Apply Rolle's theorem or the Mean Value Theorem to a given function and interval to find, or prove the existence of, the guaranteed point c."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a limit exists just because the function is defined at that point — a limit depends on the function's behavior approaching the point from both sides, which must agree independent of the actual value f(a).","Assuming continuity implies differentiability — continuity only rules out breaks or jumps; a function can be continuous but have a sharp corner, like |x| at 0, where no single tangent line exists.","Misapplying the product or quotient rule by differentiating each factor separately and multiplying the results — d/dx[f·g] is f'g + fg', not f'·g', and skipping the cross terms is a common mechanical error.","Applying Rolle's theorem without checking all three hypotheses — continuity on the closed interval, differentiability on the open interval, and equal endpoint values — since if even one fails, the guaranteed point c may not exist."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Let f(x) = x³ − 3x on [0, 2]. Verify f satisfies the Mean Value Theorem's hypotheses on this interval, then find the value of c guaranteed by the theorem.","steps":["Check hypotheses: f(x) = x³−3x is a polynomial, so it is continuous on [0,2] and differentiable on (0,2) — both MVT hypotheses hold.","Compute f(0) = 0³−3(0) = 0, and f(2) = 2³−3(2) = 8−6 = 2.","Compute the average rate of change over [0,2]: [f(2)−f(0)]/(2−0) = 2/2 = 1.","Compute the derivative: f'(x) = 3x² − 3.","Set f'(c) equal to the average rate of change and solve: 3c²−3 = 1 → 3c² = 4 → c² = 4/3 → c = ±2/√3 = ±2√3/3 ≈ ±1.1547.","Discard the negative root since c must lie in (0,2): c = 2√3/3 ≈ 1.1547."],"result":"The Mean Value Theorem guarantees c ≈ 1.1547 in (0,2) where f'(c) equals the average rate of change of 1 — confirmed by direct substitution: f'(1.1547) = 3(1.1547)²−3 ≈ 1."}}
{"title":"Calculus & Optimization Basics","summary":"Calculus & Optimization Basics applies differentiation to the practical problem of finding the best (maximum or minimum) value of a function, and introduces integration as the reverse process of differentiation used to compute accumulated quantities like area. A function has a local extremum at a critical point, where f'(x)=0 or f' is undefined, and the first derivative test (checking whether f' changes sign around the point) or the second derivative test (f''(x)>0 means local minimum, f''(x)<0 means local maximum, f''(x)=0 is inconclusive) classifies which kind it is; on a closed interval, the global maximum or minimum must occur either at a critical point or at an endpoint, which is the basis of the standard optimization-problem recipe. Integration reverses differentiation: the indefinite integral ∫f(x)dx is a family of antiderivatives differing by a constant C, while the definite integral ∫[a,b] f(x)dx computes the net signed area between the curve and the x-axis over [a,b]. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links the two halves of the subject: if F is an antiderivative of f, then ∫[a,b] f(x)dx = F(b) − F(a), meaning areas can be computed exactly through antiderivatives rather than by taking limits of sums directly. These techniques are the mathematical foundation for optimization problems throughout CS — from minimizing a loss function in machine learning to resource-allocation problems in operations research — and integration specifically underlies probability, where probabilities are computed as areas under continuous density curves.","learning_outcomes":["Find all critical points of a differentiable function and classify each as a local maximum, local minimum, or neither, using the first or second derivative test.","Solve a constrained optimization word problem, such as maximizing area or minimizing cost, by setting up an objective function, reducing it to one variable, and applying calculus to find the optimum.","Evaluate a definite integral using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, and interpret the result as a signed area."],"common_mistakes":["Treating every critical point as an extremum without checking further — f'(x)=0 is necessary but not sufficient, since a point like x=0 for f(x)=x³ has f'(0)=0 but is an inflection point, not a max or min.","Forgetting to check endpoints when finding a global extremum on a closed interval — the derivative tests only classify local behavior at interior critical points, and the true global max or min can sit at an endpoint instead.","Dropping the constant of integration (+C) on indefinite integrals, or mistakenly adding one to a definite integral — a definite integral evaluates to a specific number and needs no such constant.","Interpreting a definite integral as always giving a positive area — ∫f(x)dx over a region where f is negative contributes negatively (signed area), so total geometric area requires integrating |f(x)| or splitting the interval at the curve's zeros first."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A farmer has 200 meters of fencing to enclose a rectangular field. Find the dimensions that maximize the enclosed area.","steps":["Let the rectangle have width x and length y. The perimeter constraint gives 2x + 2y = 200, so y = 100 − x.","Write area as a function of x alone: A(x) = xy = x(100−x) = 100x − x².","Differentiate: A'(x) = 100 − 2x.","Set A'(x) = 0 to find the critical point: 100 − 2x = 0 → x = 50.","Confirm a maximum using the second derivative: A''(x) = −2, negative for all x, so x=50 gives a maximum.","Compute y at this x: y = 100 − 50 = 50, and the maximum area is A(50) = 50×50 = 2500 square meters."],"result":"The maximum-area rectangle is a 50m × 50m square, enclosing 2500 m² — illustrating that for a fixed perimeter, a square maximizes rectangular area."}}
{"title":"Linear Algebra","summary":"Linear Algebra studies vectors, matrices, and the linear transformations they represent — the mathematical backbone of computer graphics, machine learning, and systems of equations. A matrix's rank is the number of linearly independent rows, equivalently columns, it has, and it determines whether a system of linear equations Ax=b has no solution, a unique solution, or infinitely many: if rank(A) equals both the number of unknowns and the rank of the augmented matrix [A|b], the system has a unique solution; if the two ranks match but are less than the number of unknowns, there are infinitely many solutions; if the ranks differ, there is no solution. The determinant of a square matrix is a single scalar that captures whether the matrix is invertible — det(A)≠0 if and only if A is invertible, if and only if its rows are linearly independent, if and only if rank(A) equals its size n — and geometrically represents the scaling factor a linear transformation applies to volume. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors satisfy Av = λv for a nonzero vector v, meaning the transformation A only stretches v by a scalar factor λ rather than rotating it, and are found by solving the characteristic equation det(A − λI) = 0; they are central to understanding repeated application of a transformation, such as in PageRank, Markov chains, and Principal Component Analysis, where the dominant eigenvector reveals the long-run or most significant direction of the system. Gaussian elimination — row-reducing a matrix to echelon form — is the standard mechanical procedure underlying rank computation, determinant computation, matrix inversion, and solving Ax=b directly, making it the single most-used computational tool in the topic.","learning_outcomes":["Given a matrix, compute its rank via row reduction, and use it together with the augmented matrix's rank to determine whether a system Ax=b has no solution, a unique solution, or infinitely many.","Compute the determinant of a 2×2 or 3×3 matrix and use it to determine whether the matrix is invertible.","Find the eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors of a small (2×2 or 3×3) matrix by solving the characteristic equation det(A−λI)=0."],"common_mistakes":["Computing the determinant of a non-square matrix, or trying to find its eigenvalues — both are only defined for square matrices, so the question doesn't arise for rectangular ones.","Concluding a system has no solution just because rank(A) is less than the number of unknowns, without checking whether it still equals the augmented matrix's rank — that case actually gives infinitely many solutions, not zero.","Forgetting to check whether a repeated eigenvalue yields enough independent eigenvectors — a repeated eigenvalue does not always come with a full set of independent eigenvectors, which affects whether the matrix is diagonalizable.","Treating matrix multiplication as commutative — AB ≠ BA in general, unlike ordinary number multiplication, and assuming otherwise silently invalidates many matrix-algebra manipulations."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A = [[4,1],[2,3]].","steps":["Set up the characteristic equation det(A − λI) = 0, where A−λI = [[4−λ, 1],[2, 3−λ]].","Compute the determinant: (4−λ)(3−λ) − (1)(2) = 0.","Expand: 12 − 7λ + λ² − 2 = 0 → λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0.","Factor: (λ−5)(λ−2) = 0, giving eigenvalues λ₁=5 and λ₂=2.","Find the eigenvector for λ₁=5: (A−5I)v=0 gives [[−1,1],[2,−2]]v=0 → −v₁+v₂=0 → v₁=v₂, so v=(1,1).","Find the eigenvector for λ₂=2: (A−2I)v=0 gives [[2,1],[2,1]]v=0 → 2v₁+v₂=0 → v₂=−2v₁, so v=(1,−2)."],"result":"A has eigenvalues λ=5 with eigenvector (1,1), and λ=2 with eigenvector (1,−2) — verified directly: A·(1,1)ᵀ = (4+1, 2+3)ᵀ = (5,5)ᵀ = 5·(1,1)ᵀ."}}
{"title":"Boolean Algebra","summary":"Boolean Algebra is the algebraic system underlying all digital logic design, built on two values (0 and 1) and three basic operations — AND (·), OR (+), and NOT (complement) — that obey laws analogous to (but distinct from) ordinary algebra: commutative, associative, and distributive laws hold, but so do idempotent laws (A+A=A), absorption (A+AB=A), and De Morgan's theorems ((A+B)'=A'B' and (AB)'=A'+B'), the single most-used identity for simplifying and implementing circuits. Every Boolean function can be written in two canonical forms: sum-of-minterms (SOP), an OR of AND terms where each minterm covers exactly one truth-table row outputting 1, and product-of-maxterms (POS), an AND of OR terms covering the 0-rows — both derived mechanically from a truth table, so any function has a guaranteed, systematic expression. The principle of duality states that swapping AND↔OR and 0↔1 in any valid identity produces another valid identity, which is why laws come in pairs. These manipulations matter because a raw canonical expression is rarely the cheapest circuit — algebraic simplification (or Karnaugh maps) reduces literal and gate count before a circuit is built, directly lowering cost, power, and propagation delay in real hardware.","learning_outcomes":["Simplify a Boolean expression to a minimal equivalent form using algebraic laws (De Morgan's, absorption, distributive, idempotent), showing each step.","Given a truth table, derive both the canonical SOP (minterm) and canonical POS (maxterm) expressions for the function.","Apply the duality principle to generate the dual of a given valid Boolean identity and verify it independently."],"common_mistakes":["Applying De Morgan's theorem to only part of an expression, leaving a stray bar over a compound term — the complement must be distributed across every literal inside the parentheses, flipping every AND to OR as it goes.","Confusing a minterm with a maxterm — a minterm is an AND term true for exactly one input combination (for 1-rows), a maxterm is an OR term false for exactly one combination (for 0-rows); mixing them gives an expression with the wrong polarity.","Assuming Boolean distributivity works only one way like ordinary arithmetic — A+BC=(A+B)(A+C) is also a valid identity with no arithmetic analogue, and forgetting this form blocks several simplification paths.","Treating simplification as optional after writing the canonical form — an unsimplified canonical expression is valid but usually far more expensive, so skipping simplification directly costs extra gates."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Simplify F = AB + AB' + A'C to its minimal form using algebraic laws.","steps":["Group the first two terms: AB + AB' = A(B+B').","Apply the complement law B+B'=1: A(B+B') = A.","Substitute back: F = A + A'C.","Apply the identity X + X'Y = X + Y, derived from distributivity: A+A'C = (A+A')(A+C) = 1·(A+C) = A+C."],"result":"F = A + C — reduced from 3 AND terms (6 literals) to 2 literals joined by OR; verified by matching truth-table outputs of both forms across all 8 input combinations of A,B,C."}}
{"title":"Logic Gates","summary":"Logic Gates are the physical (or simulated) hardware building blocks that implement Boolean operations — AND, OR, and NOT are fundamental, while NAND, NOR, XOR, and XNOR are derived gates, each with its own symbol and truth table. NAND and NOR are called universal gates because either alone is sufficient to implement any Boolean function — any AND, OR, or NOT can be built using only NAND gates (or only NOR gates) — which is why real chip fabrication standardizes heavily on NAND-based cell libraries. XOR outputs 1 only when its inputs differ, making it the core of binary adders (it computes the sum bit, ignoring carry), while XNOR is its complement, outputting 1 when inputs match, useful for equality/parity checking. Every real gate has a non-zero propagation delay — the time between an input changing and the output settling — and this accumulates through a chain of gates, which is why circuit depth (not just gate count) determines how fast a circuit can run. Understanding gates is the bridge between Boolean algebra (the abstract math) and combinational circuits (the actual multi-gate designs), since every simplified expression must ultimately map onto a specific set of available physical gates.","learning_outcomes":["Given a Boolean expression, draw the corresponding gate-level circuit diagram using standard gate symbols.","Implement a given AND, OR, or NOT function using only NAND gates (or only NOR gates), demonstrating gate universality.","Given a multi-gate circuit and per-gate propagation delays, compute the circuit's total propagation delay along its critical (longest) path."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming NAND and NOR behave like 'AND/OR with a bubble' in circuit composition — chaining gates to build universal-gate implementations must be derived from truth tables, not assumed by analogy.","Confusing XOR and XNOR truth tables — XOR is 1 when inputs differ, XNOR is 1 when inputs match; swapping them silently inverts every output bit of a downstream adder or comparator.","Computing propagation delay by summing every gate in the circuit rather than only the critical path — parallel paths don't add to total delay; only the slowest input-to-output path determines when the output is valid.","Treating gate-level simplification as unnecessary once a circuit works — extra gates on the critical path directly increase delay, so an unsimplified but functionally correct diagram can still be a strictly worse design."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Using only 2-input NAND gates, implement F = A AND B, and compute total propagation delay if each NAND gate has a 2 ns delay.","steps":["NAND(A,B) = (AB)', so a single NAND gate gives the complement of AND, not AND itself.","Recover AND by inverting the NAND output; NOT(X) is built from a NAND gate by tying both inputs together: NAND(X,X) = (X·X)' = X'.","Build the circuit: gate 1 computes N1 = (AB)'; gate 2 takes N1 as both inputs, computing N2 = (N1·N1)' = N1' = AB.","Verify: A=1,B=1 → N1=(1·1)'=0; N2=(0·0)'=1, matching AB=1. Checked across all 4 input rows.","Count gates on the signal path: 2 NAND gates in series, so critical path length is 2 gates."],"result":"F = A AND B is implemented using 2 NAND gates in series, with total propagation delay 2×2ns = 4 ns — confirming gate universality and that delay depends on the longest signal path, not total gate count."}}
{"title":"Combinational Circuits","summary":"Combinational circuits are digital circuits whose output at any instant depends only on the current input values, with no memory of past inputs — this is what distinguishes them from sequential circuits, which have state. The half adder computes Sum = A XOR B and Carry = A AND B but cannot chain for multi-bit addition since it has no carry-in; the full adder fixes this by accepting a carry-in and computing Sum = A XOR B XOR Cin and Carry-out = AB + Cin(A XOR B), and chaining full adders (a ripple-carry adder) adds arbitrary-width numbers, at the cost of the carry needing to ripple through every stage before the final sum is valid. A multiplexer (MUX) selects one of 2ⁿ data inputs to route to a single output based on n select lines, while a demultiplexer (DEMUX) does the reverse; both are built from AND/OR gates and select-line decoding logic. Decoders convert an n-bit binary code into one of 2ⁿ active output lines (used for memory address decoding), encoders perform the reverse, and priority encoders resolve simultaneously active inputs by outputting the code for the highest-priority one. These blocks combine into larger units — an ALU is fundamentally a large combinational circuit built from adders, comparators, and multiplexers selecting between operations.","learning_outcomes":["Design a full adder from its Boolean equations and show how chaining full adders produces a ripple-carry adder for multi-bit addition.","Given a truth table, implement it using an appropriately-sized multiplexer as a universal function generator.","Design a decoder or encoder circuit for a specified number of inputs/outputs and trace its behavior for a given input combination."],"common_mistakes":["Using a half adder where a full adder is required — a half adder has no carry-in, so only the least-significant stage of a ripple-carry adder can safely be one.","Assuming a ripple-carry adder's output is valid the instant inputs are applied — the carry must propagate sequentially through every stage, so worst-case delay is proportional to bit-width, not a single gate delay.","Miscounting select lines for a multiplexer — an n-to-1 MUX needs exactly log2(n) select lines (an 8-to-1 MUX needs 3, not 8); using the wrong count either wastes lines or fails to address all inputs.","Confusing a decoder's output count with its input count — an n-input decoder activates one of 2ⁿ outputs, so a 3-input decoder needs 8 output lines, not 3."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Build a 2-bit ripple-carry adder from full adders and add binary 01 (1) and 11 (3), with initial carry-in C0=0.","steps":["Stage 0: A0=1,B0=1,C0=0. Sum0 = 1⊕1⊕0 = 0. Carry-out C1 = A0B0 + C0(A0⊕B0) = (1)(1)+0(0) = 1.","Stage 1: A1=0,B1=1,C1=1. Sum1 = 0⊕1⊕1 = 0. Carry-out C2 = A1B1 + C1(A1⊕B1) = (0)(1)+(1)(1) = 1.","Assemble result: C2 Sum1 Sum0 = 1 0 0 (binary) = 4 (decimal)."],"result":"01 + 11 = 100 in binary (1+3=4 in decimal) — the carry generated in stage 0 correctly rippled into and affected stage 1's sum, showing why every stage beyond the first needs a full adder's carry-in."}}
{"title":"Sequential Circuits","summary":"Sequential circuits are digital circuits whose output depends not only on current inputs but also on a stored internal state, which gives them memory and fundamentally separates them from combinational circuits. The basic memory element is the latch (level-triggered, like the SR or D latch, which follows its input whenever an enable signal is high) and the flip-flop (edge-triggered, sampling input only at a clock edge), with flip-flops being standard in clocked design because edge-triggering avoids the timing hazards of level-sensitive latches. The four classic flip-flop types are SR (set-reset, with an invalid state when both S and R are 1), D (simply stores whatever is on D at the clock edge — simplest and most common), JK (a fixed version of SR where J=K=1 causes toggling instead of an invalid state), and T (toggle, flipping state every clock edge when T=1); any can be built from the others via gate-level conversions. Every flip-flop has a setup time (how long input must be stable before the clock edge) and hold time (how long after), and violating either can cause metastability — an unpredictable, unresolved output state, a real hardware failure mode, not a theoretical concern. Sequential circuits are the building blocks for registers, counters, and finite-state machines, making it possible for digital systems to have memory and synchronization at all.","learning_outcomes":["Given a flip-flop type (SR, D, JK, or T) and a sequence of inputs across clock edges, construct the resulting state/output timing sequence.","Distinguish latches from flip-flops by identifying level-triggered vs edge-triggered behavior, and explain the hazard this distinction avoids.","Convert one flip-flop type into another (e.g., implement a T flip-flop using a JK flip-flop) by deriving the required input logic."],"common_mistakes":["Treating latches and flip-flops as interchangeable — a latch is level-triggered and can change output whenever enable is active, while a flip-flop samples only at a clock edge; assuming edge-triggered behavior from a latch causes unpredictable circuits.","Forgetting SR flip-flops have an invalid input combination (S=R=1) — unlike JK, which resolves the equivalent case into a toggle, S=R=1 leaves an SR flip-flop's output undefined.","Ignoring setup and hold time constraints in a timing diagram — an input changing too close to the clock edge can cause metastability, where the output takes an unpredictably long time to settle, not an instantaneous transition.","Assuming a T flip-flop and a JK flip-flop with J=K tied together behave identically without deriving it — the equivalence must be explicitly constructed since a T flip-flop has only one input."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A JK flip-flop starts at Q=0. Trace Q after 4 rising clock edges given (J,K) = (1,0), (0,0), (1,1), (0,1) at edges 1-4.","steps":["JK rules: J=0,K=0→hold; J=1,K=0→set Q=1; J=0,K=1→reset Q=0; J=1,K=1→toggle Q.","Edge 1 (1,0)=set: Q becomes 1.","Edge 2 (0,0)=hold: Q stays 1.","Edge 3 (1,1)=toggle: Q flips from 1 to 0.","Edge 4 (0,1)=reset: Q becomes 0 (already 0)."],"result":"Q sequence across the 4 edges: 1, 1, 0, 0 — demonstrating all four JK behaviors (set, hold, toggle, reset) in one traced sequence."}}
{"title":"Karnaugh Maps & Circuit Minimization","summary":"Karnaugh maps (K-maps) are a visual method for minimizing Boolean expressions by exploiting a key property: adjacent cells differ in exactly one variable, because rows and columns are ordered using Gray code (00, 01, 11, 10) rather than plain binary — this adjacency makes grouping cells equivalent to applying A+A'=1 to eliminate a variable automatically. Groups must be rectangular and contain a power-of-2 number of 1s (1, 2, 4, 8...); larger groups eliminate more variables, so minimization means covering every required 1-cell with the fewest, largest possible groups, including wraparound adjacency (edges are adjacent to the opposite edge). Don't-care conditions — input combinations that can't occur or whose output doesn't matter — are marked X and included in a group only when doing so enlarges it, since a don't-care is not a required 1. K-maps are practical for 2 to 4 variables (5 is workable with paired maps but visually unwieldy) and are replaced by the algebraic Quine-McCluskey tabulation method or automated tools for larger variable counts. The output of a correctly minimized K-map is a minimal sum-of-products (or product-of-sums, grouping 0s instead) — the same guarantee canonical forms provide, but with minimum literal count, translating directly into fewer gates.","learning_outcomes":["Construct a correctly Gray-code-ordered K-map (2, 3, or 4 variables) from a truth table or Boolean expression.","Identify valid groupings on a K-map (power-of-2 size, rectangular, including wraparound) covering all required 1-cells with the fewest, largest groups.","Incorporate don't-care cells into groupings only when they enlarge a group, and read off the resulting minimal SOP expression."],"common_mistakes":["Ordering K-map rows/columns in plain binary instead of Gray code — this breaks the one-variable-difference adjacency the method depends on, so groupings drawn don't correspond to valid simplifications.","Forming a group that isn't a power of 2 in size — non-power-of-2 groups don't correspond to a valid elimination of variables and produce an incorrect term.","Forgetting wraparound adjacency — missing this causes more, smaller groups than necessary, producing a valid but non-minimal expression.","Including every don't-care in a group regardless of benefit — a don't-care should only be circled when it enlarges a group; including one that doesn't help adds complexity without reducing literal count."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Minimize F(A,B,C,D) = Σm(0,2,8,10,11) using a 4-variable K-map (bit order A,B,C,D).","steps":["Plot the 5 minterms with rows ordered by AB and columns by CD, both in Gray code.","The four corners (m0,m2,m8,m10) are adjacent via wraparound and all share B=0,D=0 while A,C vary freely — a valid 4-cell group: term B'D'.","The remaining minterm m11 (A=1,B=0,C=1,D=1) is adjacent to m10; together they share A=1,B=0,C=1 while D varies — a valid 2-cell group: term AB'C.","Check if m11's group can extend to 4 cells: doing so would require including m9, which is not in the minterm list and must be 0 — so 2 cells is the largest valid group containing m11.","Combine with OR: F = B'D' + AB'C."],"result":"F(A,B,C,D) = B'D' + AB'C — a 5-literal, 2-term minimal SOP using wraparound to combine the four map corners into a single term, verified to cover exactly {0,2,8,10,11}."}}
{"title":"Number Systems & Data Representation","summary":"Number Systems & Data Representation covers how computers encode numeric and character data using a fixed number of bits, and the trade-offs different encodings make. Unsigned binary represents only non-negative integers directly as base-2 place values, while signed integers use sign-magnitude (a dedicated sign bit plus magnitude, with two representations of zero), 1's complement (negate by flipping every bit, also two zeros), or 2's complement (negate by flipping every bit and adding 1) — 2's complement is what virtually all real hardware uses because it has a single zero and lets addition/subtraction use the same adder circuit regardless of operand sign. For an n-bit 2's complement number, the representable range is −2^(n−1) to 2^(n−1)−1, and overflow occurs when a result falls outside this range, detected by comparing the carry into and out of the sign bit. Real numbers use floating point, standardized by IEEE 754, splitting a number into a sign bit, a biased exponent (an unsigned field representing both positive and negative true exponents), and a mantissa with an implicit leading 1 for normalized numbers — trading exact precision for enormous range, which is why floating-point arithmetic is not associative and exact-equality comparisons are generally unreliable. Character data is encoded separately (ASCII maps 128 characters to 7-bit codes, extended by Unicode/UTF-8 for world scripts), and understanding these representations is foundational to arithmetic circuit design and to why floating-point rounding bugs are a real, recurring source of software errors.","learning_outcomes":["Convert a number between binary, octal, decimal, and hexadecimal, and encode/decode a signed integer using 2's complement.","Given two n-bit 2's complement numbers, perform binary addition/subtraction and correctly detect whether signed overflow occurred.","Given an IEEE 754 single-precision representation, decompose it into sign, exponent, and mantissa and compute the represented decimal value (or vice versa)."],"common_mistakes":["Computing 2's complement negation by only flipping bits (which is 1's complement) and forgetting to add 1 — a common error that produces a value off by exactly one from the correct negation.","Assuming overflow is detected from the final carry-out of the whole addition — signed overflow is instead detected by comparing carry-into and carry-out-of the sign bit specifically; unsigned carry-out and signed overflow can disagree.","Treating the IEEE 754 exponent field as a plain signed integer rather than a biased value — the stored exponent has a fixed bias (127 for single precision) subtracted to get the true exponent; skipping the bias gives a wildly wrong value.","Comparing two floating-point numbers for exact equality after arithmetic — rounding error means mathematically equal expressions can produce bit-different results, so equality checks need a tolerance instead."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Using 4-bit 2's complement (range −8 to 7), compute 5 + 4 and determine whether signed overflow occurs.","steps":["Represent 5: 0101. Represent 4: 0100.","Add bit by bit from LSB: pos0 1+0=1; pos1 0+0=0; pos2 1+1=10→write0 carry1; pos3(sign) 0+0+carry1=1, carry-out=0.","Result bits: 1001. Carry into sign bit = 1, carry out of sign bit = 0 — they differ, so signed overflow occurred.","Decode 1001 as 4-bit 2's complement: flip→0110, add1→0111=7, so 1001 represents −7."],"result":"Signed overflow is flagged (1≠0) — correctly catching that 5+4=9 doesn't fit in 4-bit range (−8 to 7); the raw bit pattern misleadingly decodes as −7 if overflow is ignored."}}
{"title":"Registers & the ALU","summary":"Registers are small, extremely fast storage locations built directly into the CPU from flip-flops, holding values the processor is actively working with, since accessing a register is far faster than accessing main memory. Key special-purpose registers include the Program Counter (PC), holding the address of the next instruction to fetch; the Instruction Register (IR), holding the instruction currently being decoded and executed; and the Memory Address Register (MAR) and Memory Buffer/Data Register (MBR), holding the address being accessed and the data being transferred, respectively — alongside these, general-purpose registers hold operands and intermediate results for the ALU. The Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) is the combinational circuit performing arithmetic (addition, subtraction) and logic (AND, OR, XOR, NOT, shifts) operations, selecting which operation via control signals from the control unit, and is typically built around an adder, since subtraction can be done via 2's complement addition. After every ALU operation, status flags summarize the result: Zero (result is 0), Carry (an unsigned operation produced a carry/borrow out of the MSB), Overflow (a signed operation's true result doesn't fit the destination width), and Sign (set to the result's MSB). These flags let the CPU implement conditional branching — a compare instruction is typically a subtraction whose result is discarded but whose flags are kept, and a subsequent conditional jump reads those flags to decide whether to branch.","learning_outcomes":["Trace register-level data movement (PC, IR, MAR, MBR, general-purpose registers) during the fetch step of the instruction cycle.","Given two binary operands and an ALU operation, compute the result and determine the resulting Zero, Carry, Overflow, and Sign flags.","Explain how a compare instruction followed by a conditional jump uses ALU status flags to implement branching, and trace flag values for a given comparison."],"common_mistakes":["Confusing the Carry flag with the Overflow flag — Carry reflects unsigned overflow (carry out of the MSB), Overflow reflects signed overflow (carry into vs out of the sign bit); an operation can set one without the other.","Assuming MAR and MBR always change together — MAR holds the address for the current memory access and MBR holds the data, loaded and used at different points in the sequence, not simultaneously as one unit.","Forgetting a compare instruction discards its arithmetic result and keeps only the flags — its entire purpose is setting flags for a following conditional jump without disturbing register contents.","Assuming the ALU updates flags only on 'compare' instructions — flags are a side effect on every ALU operation, which is what makes flag values after an arbitrary arithmetic instruction meaningful for later branching too."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Using 8-bit signed (2's complement) registers, add 100 + 50 and determine the resulting Zero, Carry, Overflow, and Sign flags.","steps":["Represent 100: 01100100. Represent 50: 00110010.","Add bit by bit from LSB: results in bits 10010110, with a carry out of bit 6 into bit 7, and carry out of bit 7 (the sign bit) = 0.","Unsigned value of 10010110 = 150, matching 100+50=150 correctly.","Signed (2's complement) interpretation of 10010110: sign bit=1 (negative); decode by flipping and adding 1 → 01101010 = 106, so the signed value is −106."],"result":"Carry=0 (no unsigned carry out of bit 7 — 150 fits unsigned), Overflow=1 (carry into sign bit=1 ≠ carry out=0 — the signed result doesn't fit), Sign=1, Zero=0 — showing Carry and Overflow test different things and can disagree: the same bits are a valid unsigned result (150) but an overflowed signed one (would misread as −106)."}}
{"title":"CPU Architecture & Instruction Cycle","summary":"CPU architecture describes how a processor is organized to execute instructions, and the instruction cycle — fetch, decode, execute (and, in most real processors, a periodic interrupt check) — is the repeating sequence every instruction goes through. In fetch, the CPU reads the instruction pointed to by the PC from memory into the Instruction Register and increments the PC; in decode, the control unit interprets the opcode to determine the operation and operand sources; in execute, the ALU or other functional unit carries out the operation, with results written back to a register or memory. The von Neumann architecture uses a single shared memory and bus for instructions and data, which is simple but creates the 'von Neumann bottleneck' since instruction and data access can't happen simultaneously over the same bus; the Harvard architecture uses physically separate memories and buses for each, avoiding this at the cost of complexity, and is common in embedded/DSP chips and as the model for a CPU's separate instruction and data caches even within an overall von-Neumann-style main memory. Instructions follow a format containing an opcode and operand fields, connecting to the RISC-vs-CISC philosophy: RISC favors a small set of simple, fixed-length, single-cycle-friendly instructions, while CISC favors fewer, more powerful, often variable-length, multi-cycle instructions doing more per instruction. Understanding the instruction cycle is the prerequisite for pipelining, since a pipelined CPU overlaps the fetch/decode/execute stages of consecutive instructions rather than running one instruction fully before starting the next.","learning_outcomes":["Trace the fetch-decode-execute cycle for a simple instruction, identifying which register (PC, IR, MAR, MBR) is involved at each stage.","Explain the von Neumann bottleneck and identify how the Harvard architecture's separate instruction/data paths avoid it.","Given an instruction format (opcode + operand fields), decode a raw instruction word into its constituent opcode and operands."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming the PC holds the address of the currently-executing instruction — it holds the address of the next instruction to fetch, and is typically incremented during fetch, before the current instruction finishes executing.","Treating von Neumann and Harvard as differing only in performance — the defining difference is structural (shared vs. separate memory/bus for instructions and data); avoiding the bottleneck is a consequence, not the definition.","Assuming RISC always uses fewer total clock cycles than CISC for the same task — RISC instructions are individually simpler, but a task may need more of them, so total cycle count isn't automatically lower.","Conflating decode with execute — decode only determines the operation and operands (control-signal generation), while execute is where the ALU actually performs it; treating interpretation and performance as the same step obscures where computation happens."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A CPU has PC=200, and memory address 200 holds 'ADD R1, R2, R3' (R1←R2+R3). Before execution, R2=15, R3=27. Trace fetch-decode-execute.","steps":["Fetch: MAR←PC (200); instruction at 200 is read into IR; PC is incremented to 201.","Decode: control unit reads IR's opcode as ADD, identifies operands R2, R3 (sources) and R1 (destination); control signals route R2, R3 into the ALU and configure it for addition.","Execute: ALU reads R2 (15) and R3 (27), computes 15+27=42.","Write-back: result 42 is written into R1."],"result":"After one cycle, R1=42 (15+27), PC=201, and R2/R3 remain unchanged — only the destination register and PC change during an ADD."}}
{"title":"Assembly Language","summary":"Assembly language is a human-readable, symbolic representation of a processor's machine code, where each instruction typically corresponds to exactly one machine instruction, unlike a high-level language where one line can expand into many — this correspondence is what makes assembly useful for understanding exactly what a CPU does and for writing performance- or hardware-critical code. An instruction generally consists of a mnemonic (like ADD or MOV) and operands whose addressing mode determines interpretation: immediate addressing uses a literal constant directly, register addressing uses a register's contents, direct addressing uses an explicitly given memory address, and indirect addressing uses a register or memory location that itself holds the address of the actual operand — each mode trades flexibility against instruction size and speed. An assembler translates assembly source into machine code typically in two passes: the first builds a symbol table by scanning for labels and computing their addresses, and the second generates machine code, resolving each label reference to its now-known address. Assembly programs also contain directives (which configure the assembly process, such as reserving memory or setting an origin address, without generating machine code directly) alongside actual instructions, and labels let a program reference a location symbolically rather than tracking raw addresses by hand. Because assembly exposes registers, memory addressing, and the instruction set directly, understanding it is what makes the stack, calling conventions, and compiler-generated code legible, and it remains the layer where compiler output, debuggers, and reverse engineering operate.","learning_outcomes":["Given a short sequence of assembly instructions with defined mnemonics and addressing modes, trace the resulting register and memory state after execution.","Identify the addressing mode used by a given operand (immediate, register, direct, or indirect) and explain how the actual operand value is determined in each case.","Explain the two-pass structure of an assembler and identify what the symbol table contains after the first pass for a given labeled program."],"common_mistakes":["Confusing a mnemonic with the underlying machine code — the mnemonic is a human-readable stand-in the assembler translates into a specific binary opcode; assuming the mnemonic itself is what the CPU executes skips the translation step.","Misreading indirect addressing as direct addressing — direct addressing uses an address as the operand's location directly, while indirect addressing requires one extra memory access (a dereference) to reach the real operand via a pointer.","Assuming a forward label reference can't be resolved — this is exactly why assemblers use two passes: the first records every label's address regardless of definition order, so forward references resolve normally in the second pass.","Treating directives as if they generate executable instructions — directives configure the assembly process (memory layout, symbol definitions) and typically produce no corresponding machine instruction, unlike a real opcode-bearing instruction."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A program starting at address 100: '100: LOAD R1, #5' (immediate); '101: LOAD R2, #10' (immediate); '102: ADD R3, R1, R2' (register); '103: STORE R3, [200]' (direct). Trace register/memory state after each instruction.","steps":["LOAD R1, #5 — immediate addressing loads literal 5 directly into R1. State: R1=5.","LOAD R2, #10 — loads literal 10 into R2. State: R1=5, R2=10.","ADD R3, R1, R2 — register addressing reads R1 and R2's current contents and sums them. State: R1=5, R2=10, R3=15.","STORE R3, [200] — direct addressing writes R3's value to explicit memory address 200."],"result":"Final state: R1=5, R2=10, R3=15, memory[200]=15 — the trace exercises three distinct addressing modes (immediate, register, direct), each interpreted differently."}}
{"title":"Memory Hierarchy & Caching","summary":"The memory hierarchy arranges storage into levels trading capacity and cost for speed — registers, then cache (often L1/L2/L3), then main memory (RAM), then secondary storage — each level roughly an order of magnitude slower and larger than the one above, working because real programs exhibit locality: temporal locality (recently accessed data is likely accessed again soon, like a loop variable) and spatial locality (data near a recent access is likely accessed soon too, like the next array element), which caching exploits by keeping recent and nearby data close to the CPU. A cache is organized into lines (fixed-size blocks) and uses a placement policy: direct-mapped (each block maps to exactly one line, simple but prone to conflict misses), fully-associative (a block can go anywhere, minimizing conflict misses at the cost of expensive parallel-search hardware), and set-associative (a middle ground most real caches use). An access is a hit if requested data is present, a miss otherwise, with misses classified as compulsory (first-ever access), capacity (cache too small), or conflict (a placement collision evicts needed data) — and Average Memory Access Time (AMAT) = Hit Time + Miss Rate × Miss Penalty quantifies overall performance. A write-through policy immediately updates both cache and main memory (simpler, always consistent, more memory traffic), while write-back updates only the cache and defers writing until eviction (tracked via a dirty bit), reducing traffic at the cost of added complexity. Because CPU speed has historically grown faster than memory speed, how well a program's access pattern exploits locality is often the dominant factor in real-world performance, more so than raw instruction count.","learning_outcomes":["Given a memory access sequence and a specified cache configuration (direct-mapped, set-associative, or fully-associative), trace which accesses are hits and which are misses.","Classify a given cache miss as compulsory, capacity, or conflict based on the access pattern and cache configuration that produced it.","Given hit time, miss rate, and miss penalty, compute Average Memory Access Time (AMAT), including for a multi-level cache hierarchy."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a larger cache always eliminates conflict misses — cache size affects capacity misses, but conflict misses depend on associativity, so a bigger direct-mapped cache can still suffer heavy conflict misses only higher associativity would fix.","Forgetting a write-back cache needs a dirty bit to track modified lines — without it, an evicted modified line would be silently discarded instead of written back, corrupting data instead of just costing time.","Computing multi-level AMAT using only the top-level cache's miss rate — a correct calculation must account for the L1 miss penalty itself including L2 access time (and so on), not treat the miss penalty as one flat constant.","Confusing temporal and spatial locality — temporal locality is reusing the same data soon, spatial locality is using nearby data soon; a program can exhibit one strongly without the other, and cache line size specifically exploits spatial locality."],"worked_example":{"setup":"An L1 cache has 2 ns hit time and 90% hit rate; an L2 cache has 8 ns hit time and 95% hit rate; main memory access takes 100 ns. Compute overall AMAT.","steps":["L1 Miss Rate = 1 − 0.90 = 0.10.","L2 Miss Rate = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05.","L1 Miss Penalty = L2 Hit Time + L2 Miss Rate × main memory time = 8 + (0.05×100) = 8+5 = 13 ns.","AMAT = L1 Hit Time + L1 Miss Rate × L1 Miss Penalty = 2 + (0.10×13) = 2+1.3 = 3.3 ns."],"result":"Overall AMAT = 3.3 ns — much closer to the 2 ns L1 hit time than the 100 ns main-memory access time, showing how a tuned cache hierarchy hides most of memory's latency even though main memory is 50x slower than L1."}}
{"title":"Pipelining & Instruction-Level Parallelism","summary":"Pipelining increases instruction throughput by overlapping the execution of multiple instructions, splitting processing into stages — the classic five-stage RISC pipeline is Instruction Fetch (IF), Instruction Decode (ID), Execute (EX), Memory access (MEM), and Write-back (WB) — so while one instruction fetches, the previous one decodes, and the one before that executes, letting the CPU work on up to five instructions simultaneously in different stages. In an ideal n-stage pipeline, theoretical speedup approaches n for a long-running program (throughput nears one instruction completed per cycle instead of one every n cycles), but hazards prevent reaching this ideal. A structural hazard occurs when two instructions need the same hardware resource simultaneously, typically resolved by duplicating the resource or stalling; a data hazard occurs when an instruction needs a result a previous, still-in-flight instruction hasn't finished computing, commonly resolved through forwarding/bypassing (routing the result directly from the producing stage to the consuming stage before official write-back) or, when forwarding can't help (like right after a load), by stalling. A control hazard arises from branches, since the pipeline doesn't know the next instruction to fetch until the branch resolves several stages later, mitigated by branch prediction (speculatively continuing, then flushing on misprediction) or stalling until resolution. Because hazards force stalls (bubbles) that reduce throughput below ideal, real pipeline performance depends on how well a specific instruction sequence and hardware design avoid or mitigate these three hazard types, which is also why compilers reorder instructions to reduce hazard-induced stalls.","learning_outcomes":["Given a sequence of instructions with data dependencies, identify which pairs create data hazards and determine whether forwarding alone resolves them or a stall is also required.","Draw or describe a pipeline diagram for a short instruction sequence across clock cycles, showing which stage each instruction occupies each cycle, including stall bubbles.","Explain the difference between structural, data, and control hazards and give a concrete instruction sequence triggering each type."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming pipelining reduces the execution time (latency) of a single instruction — pipelining improves throughput, not the latency of any individual instruction, which still takes the same number of stages/cycles to fully complete.","Assuming forwarding alone resolves every data hazard — a load-use hazard still requires at least one stall cycle because the loaded value isn't available until after the MEM stage completes, regardless of forwarding paths.","Treating a branch misprediction as free once detected — flushing incorrectly-fetched instructions and restarting costs real bubble cycles proportional to how many stages occurred before the misprediction was caught.","Confusing a control hazard with a data hazard when a branch condition itself depends on a not-yet-computed value — this is a data hazard feeding into a control hazard, and treating it as pure control-flow misses that forwarding the condition value earlier reduces the resulting penalty."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Two instructions on a 5-stage pipeline (IF,ID,EX,MEM,WB): Instr1 'ADD R1,R2,R3' then Instr2 'SUB R4,R1,R5' (needs R1's new value), with EX-to-EX forwarding available.","steps":["Cycle 1: Instr1 in IF.","Cycle 2: Instr1 in ID; Instr2 in IF.","Cycle 3: Instr1 in EX (computes R1, result ready end of cycle); Instr2 in ID.","Cycle 4: Instr1 in MEM; Instr2 in EX — the EX-to-EX forwarding path delivers Instr1's result directly into Instr2's EX input, so no stall is needed.","Cycle 5: Instr1 in WB; Instr2 in MEM.","Cycle 6: Instr2 in WB."],"result":"With EX-to-EX forwarding, both instructions complete in 6 cycles with zero stall bubbles, versus 10 cycles if run fully sequentially (5 cycles each) — without forwarding, Instr2 would have to stall in ID until Instr1's WB completed."}}
